e database has to scan `users` and lowercase every row. That fits \"slow since last week\" if `users` grew past a threshold, or if someone added the `lower()` recently. I only have the query, not the schema or a plan, so this is an inference.\n\n**Fixes, in the order I'd try them:**\n\n1. **Add an expression index that matches the query:**\n   ```sql\n   CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_users_email_lower ON users (lower(email));\n   ```\n   This is Postgres syntax, and `CONCURRENTLY` avoids locking writes. MySQL 8 uses `((lower(email)))`.\n2. **Check that `orders.user_id` is indexed.** Once the user lookup is fast, the join needs this index. Without it, the join scans all of `orders` for that user.\n3. **Long term, normalize emails on write.** You can store them lowercased or use `citext` in Postgres. Then the query can use `u.email = ...` with an ordinary index.\n4. **Replace `SELECT *`** with only the columns the page renders. It's probably not the 8-second cause, but it widens rows, and it can stop index-only scans. It also means any new wide column on either table, like a JSON blob, ends up in this query.\n\n**To confirm before changing anything**, run this:\n```sql\nEXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) SELECT * FROM orders o JOIN users u ON o.user_id = u.id WHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';\n```\nIf you see a `Seq Scan on users` with a filter on `lower(email)`, that confirms it. If `users` uses an index but `orders` has a `Seq Scan`, the missing `user_id` index is the problem.\n\nIf the plan looks fine, the slowness is probably outside this query. Possibilities are an N+1 pattern on the page, or the page loading this query's results many times. Paste the plan or the schema and I'll look at it. I can also check the repo's migrations to see what changed last week.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}